+++ b/docs/memory/decision-log/dl-002-json-patch-clone-on-copy-path-accepted-as-root-target-varies-by-operation.md
@@ -0,0 +1,41 @@
+---
+id: dl-002-json-patch-clone-on-copy-path-accepted-as-root-target-varies-by-operation
+type: decision-log
+title: "JSON Patch: clone on copy, path accepted as root target varies by operation"
+status: pending
+---
+
+## Context
+
+Implementing `applyPatch` (RFC 6902) surfaced two points the RFC leaves to the implementation:
+
+1. RFC 6902 section 4.5 says `copy` "copies" the value at `from` to `path`, but does not say
+   whether, in an implementation whose JSON values are mutable objects/arrays (as in this
+   project's `JsonValue`), the copy may share structure with its source. If it did, a later
+   operation on either the original or the copy — e.g. an `add` appending an array element at the
+   copy's location — would silently mutate both.
+2. RFC 6902 does not explicitly define what happens when a `path` of `""` (the pointer to the
+   whole document) is used with each operation. `add`/`replace`/`move`/`copy` naturally support it
+   (the whole document is "a value to add/replace", or a valid `add` target for `move`/`copy`);
+   `remove` does not, because `applyPatch`'s signature must always return a `JsonValue`, and there
+   is no `JsonValue` that means "no document".
+
+## Decision
+
+- `copy` deep-clones the value at `from` before inserting it at `path` (see `applyCopy` in
+  `src/index.ts`), so a copy never shares structure with its source. `move` does not clone: the
+  value it reinserts has already been detached from the document by the preceding `remove`, so no
+  other reference to it remains.
+- `path: ""` (or `from: ""`) is accepted as "the whole document" for `add`, `replace`, `move` and
+  `copy`, matching RFC 6902 section 4.1's explicit root case for `add` and section 4.3's
+  definition of `replace` as `remove` + `add`. A `remove` operation with `path: ""` throws
+  (`applyRemove`), since removing the whole document has no representable result under this
+  library's `JsonValue`-returning signature.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- Callers can rely on a `copy`'d subtree being independent of its source; mutating one through a
+  later patch operation never affects the other.
+- A patch that tries `{"op": "remove", "path": ""}` fails fast with a clear error instead of
+  returning an ambiguous value (e.g. `null`, which would be indistinguishable from a document that
+  legitimately is `null`).
